---
// Goal: take a reader from the install line to a pipeline that sorts a
// stream of messages, one idea per step, every run real.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import InstallLine from '../../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { SURFACES, KEY_VARIABLE, HOWTOS, FUNCTIONS } from '../../data/catalog.mjs';
import { captioned, files } from '../../data/samples.mjs';

const shell = SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === 'shell');
const see = (name) => FUNCTIONS.find((f) => f.name === name).see;
const decide = captioned('functions/decide', see('decide'));
const run = (name) => decide.find((r) => r.name === name);

const plain = run('3-one');
const answer = run('4-details');
const lines = run('1-lines');
const branch = run('2-case');

const [savedYes, savedUnsure] = captioned('functions/question-file', see('question-file'));
const refundFile = files('functions/question-file')[0];

const kept = captioned('functions/filter', see('filter'))[0];
const sorted = captioned('functions/rank', see('rank'))[0];
const triagePage = 'how-tos/triage-a-support-inbox';
const triage = captioned(triagePage, HOWTOS.find((h) => h.slug === 'triage-a-support-inbox').see);
const triageForm = files(triagePage)[0];
---
<Base title="Tutorial" description="From the install line to a pipeline that sorts a stream of messages.">
  <h1>Tutorial</h1>
  <p class="lede">From the install line to a pipeline that sorts a stream of messages. You ask one question, read the number behind the answer, set your own bar, and save the question to a file.</p>

  <h2>1. Install it</h2>
  <InstallLine line={shell.install[0][0]} caption="The download script" />
  <InstallLine line={shell.install[1][0]} caption="Homebrew, an option on a Mac" />
  <p>Set <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code> in the environment. <code>--dry-run</code> shows what a command would send and needs no key. The <a href="/install/">install page</a> covers every language and database.</p>

  <h2>2. Ask one question</h2>
  <p>Name the question, pipe the message in, and pass the question as an argument. <code>decide</code> prints <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, or <code>null</code>, and it sets the exit code to match.</p>
  <Terminal run={plain} caption={plain.see} />
  <p>The exit code is the answer. A script branches on it the way it branches on <code>test</code>.</p>

  <h2>3. Look at the number</h2>
  <p><code>true</code> hides a number. <code>--details</code> prints the whole result, and the model's probability sits under <code>answer</code>.</p>
  <Terminal run={answer} caption={answer.see} />
  <p>That number is the model's own. ThinkThen passes it through unchanged.</p>

  <h2>4. Set a band</h2>
  <p><code>--threshold</code> is your bar on the probability. One number is a cut, and the default is 0.5. Two numbers make a band: under the low bar is no, over the high bar is yes, and anything between is not sure.</p>
  <Terminal run={lines} caption={lines.see} />
  <p>The threshold never changes the probability. It changes what you call a yes.</p>

  <h2>5. Branch on the answer</h2>
  <p>Not sure prints <code>null</code> and exits 3. A script reads the exit code and hands a person the calls it should not make alone.</p>
  <Terminal run={branch} caption={branch.see} />

  <h2>6. Save the question to a file</h2>
  <p>A question worth tuning is worth keeping. A question file holds one question: the words, what a yes and a no mean, and the threshold.</p>
  <Code code={refundFile.text} caption={`${refundFile.name}, the saved question`} />
  <p>Write <code>@refund.json</code> in place of the question words.</p>
  <Terminal run={savedYes} caption={savedYes.see} />
  <Terminal run={savedUnsure} caption={savedUnsure.see} />

  <h2>7. Judge a stream</h2>
  <p>One of <code>--lines</code>, <code>--jsonl</code>, <code>--csv</code>, or <code>--tsv</code> says how the records are framed. <code>filter</code> keeps the records where the answer is yes, byte for byte and in order.</p>
  <Terminal run={kept} caption={kept.see} />
  <p><code>rank</code> sorts the records by how likely the answer is yes.</p>
  <Terminal run={sorted} caption={sorted.see} />
  <p>Put them in a pipe and the second question reads only what the first kept. <a href="/how-tos/rank-the-inbound-leads/">See the leads how-to</a> for <code>filter</code>, <code>rank</code>, and <code>choose</code> in one pipeline.</p>

  <h2>8. Ask many questions at once</h2>
  <p><code>annotate</code> fills out a form for every record. The form is a question set: any mix of <code>decide</code>, <code>choose</code>, <code>score</code>, and <code>tag</code> questions in one file.</p>
  <Code code={triageForm.text} caption={`${triageForm.name}, the question set`} />
  {triage.map((step) => <Terminal run={step} caption={step.see} />)}
  <p><code>filter</code> drops the thank-you note. Each message left comes back with one field per question.</p>

  <p>ThinkThen answers. Your code acts.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/learn/beatles-bench/">Next: Beatles Bench</a> · <a href="/how-tos/bash/">Learn the Bash techniques</a></p>
</Base>
